f a type of theme  and message no people are wrong when  they tell you that it needs to be said  in the future it needs to be science  fiction it doesn't it just needs to be  clearly you know a warning sign for hey  look where we're headed don't let it get  this far if we have like a fantasy  series where the wizards created giant  magical bombs that explode in the shape  of mushroom clouds and they let them get  out of hand and they all went to war  with one another and annihilated the  entire world and created a dystopian  society in the wake of it  come on like  that's clearly warning us about like  nuclear weapons and stuff like that  the message is clear it's still  absolutely a warning and like yeah  technically you can look at any society  that is broken if you draw a line you  say hey this is what it means to be  broken where it has severe flaws and  injustice and problems and you can say  this is a dystopia but i feel like that  robs it of  any real meaning because you look at  game of thrones and it's incredibly  unjust and awful but it's not a dystopia  it's not a helpful description of the  world it doesn't let us know what the  story is about really uh it's it's it  just it's just not something that helps  and these terms that we use in literary  circles they're about helping us they're  about helping us define stories and  describe stories and articulate stories  and so we don't use the term dystopia to  describe just societies that have  incredible issues  but can reform them or are just flawed  societies but what does broken mean what  is what does flawed mean right and  that's why it also feels really weird to  me when people say hey the united states  is a dystopia or north korea is a  dystopia  because  uh  these are again literary vehicles for  meaning and north korea are in the  united states they're not they're not uh  they're not vessels for meaning they're  describing the real world almost  inherently dystopias exist in contrast  to the real world  and that doesn't mean that like um every  part of the real world has to be worse  than the thing you're describing in a  dystopia uh i can think of a number of  stories you know brave new world um  even hunger games that technically  speaking there are there are places that  are worse out there than than the events  described it's it's the contrast and  messaging that makes it a dystopian in  many in many senses  in north korea you know it's not a story  written with intent it's it's a tragedy  of the real world but you know we have  other terms for things like that we have  dictatorships and failed states at the  same time that doesn't mean that arcane  doesn't comment on the real world that  we can't take things away from it it has  a lot to say about power and technology  and you know poverty but  uh that's not the same thing you know  all stories have some meaning of some  kind but that doesn't even if it's  saying like hey these things are wrong  about society that doesn't make it a  dystopia  so  the last thing that kind of came to mind  is that a lot of people have brought up  you know i asked you know what do you  think a dystopia is is this one people  said you know it's the appearance of the  dista of the the utopia and piltover  looks like a utopia it's wealthy in  progress and and it's cool right it's  beautiful and i get where you're coming  from  but it's it's it that that's not a  requirement for a dystopia does not have  to have the appearance of a utopia um  and that is really a more recent trope  or actually it's quite an old trope but  it's something that at least  newer readers tend to associate with the  with the genre a lot more you know with  things like the giver and and so on in  summary the mere existence of injustice  does not a dystopia make however it does  have dystopian elements all right you  know it does have things in common with  dystopias and i think that was  purposeful it draws on in in many senses  the same fears and worries and concerns  that we have in the modern day uh as a  lot of stories do and so you can draw a  lot of parallels between them which is  what i was doing in the previous video  um on arcane you can draw a lot of  parallels about uh what these stories  are saying about the real world and  you're going to find crossover between  dystopian and just like generic science  fiction or fantasy  absolutely  and i think that's what we're seeing  there so that that that's my thoughts  not dystopian has dystopian elements uh  but again  genre it's all fluid right it doesn't  make sense  things can be whatever we want them to  be
 

